Barrier-pooled virtual approximation and conditional helicity corrections
=========================================================================

New NLO exports enable #UsePooledVirtual in Cards/FKS_params.dat. Old cards
without that key retain the previous default. Set it false to disable pooling
and use the existing UsePolyVirtual selection or MINT marginals. Restart from
grid setup after changing the mode or physics inputs; never reuse an old
unweighting envelope with a changed model.

Workflow and normalization
--------------------------
Workers independently record virtuals already evaluated during MC@NLO step 0
or fixed-order NLO step 1. Only after the entire stage finishes does the
controller call train_pool(job_dirs, output_model, report_path, job_groups).
It pools integration channels and split replicas within the same actual Born
subprocess and fits each coupling order separately. There is no communication
among workers during a stage. Frozen models are copied into subsequent job
directories, including cluster transfers. Controller resumes verify checksums.
An optional virtual_pool_neural.dat companion is trained at this same barrier.
It uses the native helicity map and probabilities captured with existing loop
evaluations; it generates no additional sampled or full-sum virtual calls.
Its checksum and the base-model checksum are frozen together. Resuming a neural
fit also verifies generated amplitude sources, model parameters and native
helicity resources. Event counts, random seeds and object files are excluded.

job_groups maps each directory to its physical integration-channel batch.
Split replicas share an attempted-point denominator. Different batches have
separate denominators. Without this mapping, each directory is its own group.
The arbitrary fixed-order replica multiplier WGT_MULT is removed at capture;
all physical integration, PDF, bias and native helicity factors remain.

B is the original native Born carrier used for the virtual correction. V is
the sampled-helicity estimator, with its existing proposal weight included.
A is the contemporaneous MINT approximation, retained for reconstruction and
diagnostics. V is reconstructed as probability*residual+A, before bookkeeping
can clear a Born entry with zero residual. No additional helicity factor is
applied. All orders of a phase-space observation share its sample ID and fold.
Neither V nor B is divided by the per-point Born or by the loop probability
in the file. A training row has statistical weight

  1 / (accepted attempted points in its integration group * loop probability).

Failed cuts and unevaluated virtuals remain in the attempted-point denominator.
Rejected and uncommitted iterations are discarded. A monotonically increasing
iteration ordinal remains unique even when native MINT restarts its counter.
Born-spreading calibration restarts discard pre-calibration observations.

Model and conservative selection
--------------------------------
The candidate is A_new=(1-lambda) A_MINT + lambda B P(z). P is linear,
quadratic including all cross terms, or a validated sparse cubic.
Invariant coordinate catalog 6 is:
  log(shat/muR^2),
  log(max(abs(2*pi.pj)/shat,1e-15)) for all i<j in native Born order,
  log(shat/m^2) for distinct positive external masses in ascending order,
  log(shat/QES2), using the actual Ellis-Sexton scale passed to MadLoop,
  log(shat), with shat expressed in the native GeV-squared units.
muR is the physical runtime renormalization scale, which can differ from
the loop reference scale QES2, for example in FxFx. The absolute scale lets
massless external states describe effects of massive internal particles.
Catalog 5 remains readable with its original meaning and lacks the last two
coordinates; captures from different catalogs are never silently combined.
Coordinates do not
require a particular spin, colour representation, or particle identity.
This is a generic fitting basis, not an exact formula for arbitrary virtuals.

Scalers use fitting rows only and map nonconstant coordinates to [-1,1].
Constants and negligible columns are removed. Regression fits weighted V
through V-B*P without per-point Born division. Born/target/column normalization
keeps the small linear systems well scaled. The fixed candidate scan is:
  degree 1 or 2; ridge 1e-4,0.01,1; Huber or squared loss, with constant
  or Born-dependent noise scale; blend 0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1.
Constant-noise Huber IRLS has at most eight iterations and a residual threshold of
0.25 times the weighted mean absolute target scale. Born-noise fits divide
the regression weights by max(abs(B)/born_scale,1e-12), while retaining the
unsingular target V-B*P. Their Huber threshold follows that same noise scale
and uses at most twenty iterations. Identical consecutive IRLS weights stop
the fit early because its next linear system would be unchanged.
The validation objective is always the original
physical residual, independently of the candidate's fitting loss.

Richer candidates use degree 3, ridge 0.01 and Born-dependent squared
loss. Catalogs 6/7 compare the complete scalar coordinate set with a relative-only
variant that excludes absolute log(shat), including from its linear and
quadratic terms. CV selects between them; no particle or process identity
selects a basis. Both keep full-catalog native index numbering and bounds.
Each preserves every constant, linear and quadratic term of its chosen
coordinate set. If the dense
cubic exceeds 512 terms, a preliminary fit ranks cubic terms by normalized
coefficient magnitude; the strongest additions are retained and the capped
model is refitted. This selection is repeated inside each fitting fold,
without consulting its held-out observations. Active feature count is capped
at 18 and the temporary dense design at 40 million values, including the
final all-sample fit. Each fitting fold needs five rows per dense candidate
term. Processes outside these budgets keep the existing simpler candidates.

Before its three CV fits, each cubic must improve the best validated simpler
model's forward L1 by at least 2%, with no more than a 5% forward RMS increase.
The cubic is skipped if there is insufficient forward history or no validated
simpler reference. Passing this preliminary screen still requires all the
ordinary matched-fold, aggregate RMS and forward-validation guards below.

Three-fold CV is stratified by job/channel/iteration, keeping coupling orders
of the same point together. Each validation baseline is a frozen native MINT
reconstruction from that same fold complement, including the recorded 4-to-8
bin schedule. The stored causal A is NOT the validation baseline. Worker and
channel histories remain distinct, as in the native grids. All sampled rows,
including exact zero residuals, enter the MINT reconstruction.
An additional forward validation fits earlier whole iterations and evaluates
later iterations separately in each worker/channel. Where enough observations
are available, candidates must not increase its L1 or increase RMS by more
than 5%. Reports include both validations' maximum residuals; these are useful
diagnostics but are not estimates of an unseen supremum.

A candidate must reduce weighted L1 by more than both 2% and twice a
conservative paired standard-error estimate. Its aggregate RMS must not
increase; no individual fold may increase RMS by more than 5%. A simpler
candidate within one paired error estimate of the best L1 is preferred over
higher degree only when its RMS and forward-validation L1/RMS are
also within 5% of the best. This comparison uses the paired difference between
the two candidates, not their improvements over MINT.
The error estimate is sqrt(sum((w_i*delta_i)^2)), with
normalized weights and paired absolute-residual differences. It prevents one
large observed gain from establishing significance by itself. These tuning
diagnostics are not independent performance measurements.

At least 60 observations and ten observations in every validation fold are
required. Each fitting fold must have at least five rows per candidate
coefficient; at most 512 coefficients are allowed. Higher multiplicities or
small samples therefore use a linear model or MINT automatically. Zero
Born/virtual orders, unsuccessful fits or no admissible improvement produce an
explicit zero-blend MINT fallback. Without NumPy on the controller, no model is
installed: ordinary MINT marginals and adaptive virtual-call fractions remain
active, including on later split jobs and resumes. Resuming a previously fitted
model does not need NumPy; workers evaluate it in Fortran and retain its frozen
event envelope. Legacy captures remain format version 2. Models
without cubics remain version 2 for those captures; a selected cubic or a new
conditional capture uses model version 3.
Both model versions and coordinate catalogs 5/6/7 are read explicitly.
Earlier experimental formats are rejected; restart their integration from
grid setup before using this implementation.

Conditional helicity correction
------------------------------
New capture version 3 stores catalog 7, the complete native helicity tuples and
GOODHEL multiplicities, the actual selected/full-sum branch, selected native
probability, complete probability vector and Born momenta. Catalog 7 appends
signed, normalized epsilon contractions to catalog 6. Polynomial terms contain
at most one such odd coordinate. These orientation inputs distinguish reflected
points which share all scalar products but can have different virtuals.

The optional neural correction has one or two tanh layers, a linear skip and
one output per trained native helicity representative. Its finite target is
V-A_common, with a Born carrier multiplying the prediction. Training never
divides a target by the point's Born. Input bounds, common-model coefficients
and neural coefficients are refitted independently in three
chronological forward splits (one half, two thirds and four fifths of each
worker/channel history). Head eligibility is frozen from the earliest fitting
subset and retained in later folds and the final fit. Every split must improve physical weighted L1 and
increase RMS by no more than 5%; aggregate RMS must not increase. Mean L1 gain
must exceed both 2% and twice the largest paired error estimate. Failed fits,
insufficient heads or missing optional SciPy retain the common approximation.
Unknown or untrained heads contribute zero conditional correction.

Native Born helicity squared orders determine exactly the same probability
mixture as PickHelicityMC, including representative multiplicities and the
actual Ellis-Sexton coupling state. The original helicity draw remains inside
BinothLHA. If A_h is the prediction for its native sampled estimator, then

  A_mean = sum_h q_h A_h
  corrected = A_mean + I/p * (V_native,h - A_h).

A native full-sum branch instead subtracts A_mean. Capture and grid adaptation
reconstruct V using the actual selected subtraction, rather than A_mean. The
deferred loop saves the first fold's full prediction table, probabilities and
native context. Native worker inference uses Fortran and consumes no RNG.
Extra diagnostic full-sum research labels are not production training inputs.

Exact correction, boundaries and call budget
-------------------------------------------
The original helicity sampler and random-number generator are retained. The
estimator uses the mean/selected identity above, or its common-model limit
A + I*(V_h-A)/p, with the actual positive loop-call probability p. Models are
frozen at the barrier. Channel call probabilities continue adapting to the
measured residual errors. Native driver policies can still explicitly require
exact loops. Event generation uses a separately trained residual proposal.

Features clamp to their training boundaries. If any coordinate of an active
model lies more than 10% of its training range beyond a boundary, the bounded
polynomial is retained and the point requests an exact virtual (p=1). Hard
replacement by MINT would recreate large residuals in just those tails.
A constant coordinate
has tolerance 1e-10*max(1,abs(value)). Invalid coordinates in apply mode also
request an exact loop. The point's actual probability is used for residual
normalization, exact-V reconstruction and virtual-envelope bookkeeping.
Folded evaluations reuse the same approximation and sampled residual.
No positivity clipping or deletion of negative weights is performed.

The collection stage itself cannot benefit from the model. Short fixed-order
runs ending at step 1 only train; later integration stages consume the fit.
The implemented residual L1/RMS promotion guards do not guarantee that
unweighting efficiency will improve or remain unchanged. A promotion guard
based on residual tails or generation-envelope cost is future work; it is not
implemented. A better residual also does not guarantee fewer negative
unweighted events or a net timing gain. Full-event tests and total
fitting/inference overhead matter.

Residual proposal and early Born veto
-------------------------------------------
For MC@NLO generation, reducing the mean residual alone does not control loop
cost: a loose residual rejection bound can still require many exact virtual
evaluations per accepted event. Pooled runs therefore train a separate
factorized residual envelope on the existing MINT cells during step 1. Every
sampled exact residual updates both this envelope and the scalar bound to
cover twice the observed absolute residual weight. The loop-call probability
is removed before either update. Factors only increase, preserving coverage
of all earlier observations. Folding uses only the active cells.

The product envelope's normalization is the product of its marginal means.
Generation chooses the smaller integrated bound as its base proposal, then
performs a cheap rejection to sample the pointwise minimum of the scalar and
product envelopes. This preliminary rejection evaluates neither phase space
nor matrix elements. The ordinary exact residual and its event sign are then
computed, followed by rejection against that same minimum bound. Thus the
proposal changes sampling efficiency without changing the residual target
when the envelope covers it. The scalar comparison includes the same
factor-two observed-point margin; an underestimated scalar cannot win merely
because its update was capped.

Step 1 also learns a dimensionless Born-carrier bound kappa from its existing
exact virtual evaluations. Its observation is twice the largest absolute
split-order residual divided by the sum of absolute native Born components,
before any inverse loop-probability weighting. Only the frozen approximation
used in this stage enters the bound. Learning requests no extra virtuals and
consumes no random draws. No positive ratio evidence disables the Born veto;
a zero total Born carrier at a point uses the original exact path.

Version 4 also learns a second kappa for the carrier

  C = sum_order abs(B_order) * (1 + sum_massive_outgoing_pairs 1/v_ij)
  v_ij = sqrt((p_i.p_j)^2 - m_i^2*m_j^2) / (p_i.p_j).

This uses generic masses and momenta, without selecting a particle species.
It accommodates a finite virtual correction where a massive-pair Born weight
vanishes at threshold. Both kappas use the same existing exact observations
and factor-two margin. Screening takes the minimum of the ordinary Born,
velocity and outer bounds. The ordinary bound protects cases where the extra
carrier is less useful. An unresolved velocity bypasses the carrier veto and
evaluates the exact residual against the outer proposal. No velocity floor is
introduced. The first fold's factor is retained with the deferred context.

For a residual generation candidate, a cheap native pass uses that positive
Born carrier in each virtual split order. It builds phase space, all folds,
PDFs, bias factors and the normal event records once. The screening bound is
kappa times the sum of absolute weighted carrier/flavour contributions, plus
absolute contributions outside the residual. Signed orders therefore cannot
cancel out the screening carrier. Generation screens against the minimum of
this bound and the outer envelope before calling MadLoop. On passing, the
exact loop uses the saved first-fold Born context; each carrier record is
multiplied by its own exact split-order residual/carrier ratio. Native event
grouping and sign selection then use these corrected weights. The final-fold
context is restored, including its scale and coupling values.

The two rejections multiply to the ordinary exact residual rejection
probability: screening with T/E followed by |R|/T gives |R|/E, where
T=min(E,Born bound,velocity bound). Native FKS, flow and flavour choices are retained. The
helicity sampler is unchanged and is called after the cheap veto, so its
random draws move later in the candidate evaluation. Complete-helicity native
identity checks and statistical sampled-helicity comparisons serve different
validation purposes. Performance comparisons must count
all integration stages and the remaining cheap carrier evaluations as well
as MadLoop calls.

These are empirical bounds, not analytic upper limits. A rare peak can exceed
either learned bound. Production diagnostics therefore report overflow counts
and the proposal-weighted excess fraction, sum(max(f/bound-1,0)) /
sum(f/bound). They also report cheap proposals, phase-space attempts, evaluated
residuals, the outer proposal normalization, kappa, and Born-carrier attempts
and vetoes. The outer normalization does not include the subsequent Born
veto. Residual excess should also be assessed against the residual sector's
fraction of the total absolute integral when evaluating physics precision.

Fixed-order integration does not use the generation envelope or Born veto.
Disabling UsePooledVirtual retains the original scalar update, generation
proposal and random-number sequence. Generation reads frozen grid data and
does not rewrite shared input grids; split replicas have no mutable shared
sampling state.

Native grid envelope metadata
-----------------------------
Pooled MC grids written after step 1 append one MAX row per MINT interval and
channel for the residual factors, then one MAX row per channel for the ordinary
and velocity kappas, followed by the marker VEP 4. This marker
is separate from the virtual model/capture version. Split-grid merging takes
componentwise maxima of the factors and kappa and requires consistent markers
and row counts. The resulting product covers the observations from every replica.

Generation reads the complete VEP 4 grid, including in only_generation and
restart workflows. VEP 3 retains its original ordinary-Born denominator with
the velocity veto disabled. VEP 2 grids retain the product/minimum proposal with the
Born veto disabled because they contain no ratio evidence. Older grids
without a VEP marker retain the scalar-only fallback. Experimental VEP 1 grids are rejected because their scalar bound
does not have the margin required by minimum-envelope sampling; rerun the
envelope integration. Step 1 normally reads the preceding step-0 grid, which
has no residual-envelope block. A diagnostic workflow that manually strips
MAX rows to reconstruct such an integration grid must also remove VEP.
Changing the approximation or physical inputs requires fresh integration
and envelope training rather than reuse of any version's old bounds.

Capture and model files
-----------------------
virtual_pool_samples.dat has five header lines:
  VIRTUAL_POOL_SAMPLES 2
  6 nf norders nlegs nx
  pdg(1:nlegs)
  mass(1:nlegs)
  incoming(1:nlegs)

The first field is the feature catalog (legacy 5 or current 6).
nx is the number of Born MINT random coordinates (ndim-3). Exactly two legs
are incoming. Each subsequent row is:
  sampleid channel iteration order bins probability B V A features(1:nf) x(1:nx)

bins is 4 or 8. All numerical fields are finite, x is in [0,1], and probability
is in (0,1]. Metadata and native Born ordering must match across workers.

virtual_pool_iterations.dat uses the iteration ledger format:
  iteration accepted attemptedpoints
where accepted is 0 or 1. Missing ledgers contribute no uncommitted rows.

virtual_pool_model.dat has the same header layout, beginning with
VIRTUAL_POOL_MODEL 2. For each coupling order it then stores:
  order lambda born_scale target_scale nterms degree
  lower(1:nf)
  upper(1:nf)
  a b coefficient             [one row per term]

Feature indices are one-based; index zero means the constant 1. Thus (0,0)
is a constant, (a,0) a linear term, and (a,b) a quadratic product. The raw
Born multiplier is target_scale/born_scale times the polynomial sum.
Degree is 0,1,2; zero blend reproduces MINT exactly. Constant boundary scaling,
bounded continuation and polynomial ordering agree between Python/Fortran.

A model containing a cubic begins with VIRTUAL_POOL_MODEL 3. Its metadata
and order headers are unchanged; every term row, including lower-degree
orders in the same model, instead stores:
  a b c coefficient
Degree may also be 3. Trailing zero indices pad lower-degree terms, while
(a,b,c) with three positive indices is a cubic product. Version-2 models are
not interpreted as triples. Capture and model format versions are independent.

Models and JSON reports are written atomically. Per-job mode markers remain
0=inactive,1=collect,2=apply. A no-header fallback needs no model. Reports give
actual point counts, candidate diagnostics, selected models and fit wall time.
A subprocess manifest binds the model checksum to the integration workflow.

Conditional capture and companion interchange
-----------------------------------------------
Capture version 3 retains the five header lines above and appends nhel,
followed by nhel complete native map records, including inactive IDs:
  actual_id GOODHEL HEL(1:nlegs)
Actual IDs are one-based and sequential. Each data row retains the legacy
prefix and appends:
  full_branch selected_actualhel selected_q q(1:nhel) pBorn(0:3,1:nlegs)
Momenta are leg-major [E,px,py,pz]. Full branches use selected_actualhel=-1
and selected_q=1. Otherwise selected_q equals q(selected_actualhel). Native q
already includes GOODHEL: it must not be multiplied by multiplicity again.
An all-zero q marks an unavailable distribution; those rows remain in common
polynomial training but are excluded from conditional fitting. nhel=0 permits
geometry-only captures. Header creation waits for validated native metadata.

Catalog 7 appends (nlegs-2)*(nlegs-3)/2 signed normalized epsilon coordinates
to catalog 6, one per pair of non-anchor Born legs. The incoming pair anchors
a scattering process; the actual incoming leg and first outgoing leg anchor
a decay. Polynomial bounds for these signed inputs are fixed [-1,1]. The
common fit adds an odd-times-linear block to the even polynomial, with at
most one signed factor per term. Legacy catalogs retain their original meaning.

train_pool(..., neural=True, output_neural=...) optionally trains
virtual_pool_neural.dat. Missing NumPy/SciPy or rejected validation yields an
explicit fallback and removes any stale companion. The automatic default recipe has one
64-unit tanh layer, ridge .001, Born-noise exponent 1 and at most 400 L-BFGS
iterations, using the scalar input bank. Larger optimization budgets, two-layer
architectures and canonical/composite input banks are manually selected
research recipes; they do not change this default. Only existing native selected/full branches are labels; no extra
full-helicity evaluations are requested. The direct loss is V-Acommon-B*NN,
including signed/zero Born carriers, without constructing a target V/B.
Untrained heads have zero correction. Full branches constrain sum(q*delta),
while sampled branches constrain their selected delta.

Three chronological forward splits per worker/channel use fractions .5, 2/3
and .8. The common polynomial recipe is selected by nested guarded CV using only
earlier observations. Its coefficients, MINT baseline, input bounds and head
masks use that same earlier subset; later labels cannot select the recipe. Each fold must improve
physical L1 with at most a 5% RMS increase; aggregate RMS must not increase.
The mean L1 gain must exceed 2% and twice the largest paired gain error across
the overlapping folds. Only an accepted recipe is refitted on all committed
rows. Reports include relative true-virtual errors, all candidates, fallback
reasons and the number of additional loop calls (zero).

The companion text codec is:
  VIRTUAL_POOL_NEURAL 1
  catalog nf norders nlegs nx nhel
  pdg(1:nlegs)
  mass(1:nlegs)
  incoming(1:nlegs)
  base_model_sha256
  actual_id GOODHEL HEL(1:nlegs)                 [nhel rows]
Then for each coupling order:
  order enabled ninputs nheads width1 width2 born_scale target_scale
Disabled orders use zero counts/widths and unit scales, with no following rows.
An enabled order contains:
  kind a b c lower upper                        [ninputs rows]
  actual_id trained                            [nheads rows]
  W1                                           [ninputs rows, width1 columns]
  bias1                                        [one row]
  W2, bias2                                    [only if width2>0]
  Wout                                         [last-width rows, nheads columns]
  output_bias                                  [one row]
  affine_skip                                  [ninputs+1 rows, nheads columns]
Matrices are input-major; hidden layers use tanh. Output is linear plus the
affine skip, with ratio correction target_scale/born_scale times that output.
Input kinds, using one-based catalog/leg/native-helicity IDs, are:
  1 scaled catalog feature a; 2 raw signed catalog feature a;
  3 canonical outgoing leg a, component b=0..3; 4 crossed triple-invariant log(a,b,c);
  5 sign of that triple invariant; 6 native probability 2*q(a)-1.
Kinds 1/4 clamp training-range scaling to [-1,1], with zero for constant bounds.
Others are raw. Canonical components are [4E/Q-1,2px/Q,2py/Q,2pz/Q] in the
incoming-CM frame. Triple logs use log(max(abs((pa+pb+pc)^2)/shat,1e-15)),
with incoming momenta crossed negative. The default input bank uses scalar,
signed-orientation and native-q features; canonical/composite banks are optional.

The codec supports one/two hidden layers, at most 64 units each, 64 output
heads, 128 inputs and a complete native map of at most 65536 IDs. Training caps
probability storage at 16 million values, feature storage at 40 million, hidden
activations at 12 million, candidate configurations at four, and optional
optimization recipes at 4000 iterations. Reports give wall and process CPU
training time separately.

An accepted conditional model may additionally propose averaging predictions
over an identical-metadata outgoing-leg swap. This is a control-variate choice,
not an assumption that a diagram-restricted or decay-chain amplitude is exactly
symmetric. No transformed targets or extra loop labels are created. The trial
averages the COMPLETE common polynomial and neural prediction, remaps native
helicity IDs and q together, and compares against the original native targets
on all three forward holds. Every active order must pass the same physical L1,
RMS, 2% gain and paired-error guards relative to the unsymmetrized conditional
model. Otherwise the companion remains version 1.

Version 2 adds a permutation table immediately after the complete native
HEL/GOOD map and before the first order block:
  npermutations
  leg_permutation(1:nlegs)                      [npermutations rows]
There are at most two permutations: identity first, then optionally one
transposition of outgoing legs with equal PDG and mass. Leg indices are
one-based. The full HEL map must be closed, GOODHEL multiplicities preserved,
and enabled-head membership and trained masks closed under the swap. The
trained-head mask is frozen using the earliest forward fitting subset; extra
all-data rows cannot activate a previously unvalidated rare head. Unknown q
retains the original common approximation. Native exact-tail flags are combined
across the prediction passes. Python predict_model evaluates complete version-1
or version-2 predictions; predict_correction is the unsymmetrized correction
primitive. No particle species or process name enables a permutation.
No process name chooses a basis, model architecture or exception.

Training cost
-------------
Linear and quadratic searches cache their design, column normalization and
initial normal equations separately for each fitting partition and noise
scale. Ridge candidates share those arrays. The cache is local to one degree
and coupling order, with a conservative budget of eight million numeric
elements (about 64 MB); larger fits recompute their arrays. Bounds and targets
from another CV fold or later chronological observations never enter a cached
fit. Cubic selection retains its existing dense-design budget and independent
fold-local feature selection. Optional SciPy uses a positive-definite solve
for ridge systems; NumPy-only controllers retain their original solver.

MINT replay batches cell sums and predictions while preserving native row
addition order, iteration history, zero observations and the 4-to-8-bin
transition. Neural optimisation prepares its affine inputs and trained native
mixture once per fit. At least 32 heads and fewer than 25% nonzero mixture
entries enable grouped single-head observations; multi-head full branches
still use the complete native probability mixture. The default 64-unit network,
400-iteration cap, candidate scan, chronology and promotion guards are retained.

The standalone synthetic benchmark is:

  python3 tests/input_files/benchmark_virtual_training.py \
      --reference-ref 849759f5c --repeats 3 --output /tmp/virtual-training.json

It pins one available CPU, sets BLAS thread limits before importing NumPy and
loads reference sources into a temporary package without changing the checkout.
JSON includes CPU times and independent prediction errors. Example median CPU
seconds over three repeats, compared with the pre-optimisation trainer:

  case                          points   reference   optimised   speedup
  polynomial, four Born legs      3000       0.499       0.164       3.04
  polynomial, six Born legs       3000       2.111       0.599       3.53
  complete conditional training    900       1.781       1.480       1.20
  nonlinear correction, 16 heads   3000       1.403       1.354       1.04
  nonlinear correction, 64 heads   3000       3.677       2.027       1.81

The polynomial predictions agreed within 4e-15 on these cases. The conditional
and 16-head errors agreed exactly; the 64-head held-out relative L1/RMS changed
from 0.147985/0.166009 to 0.147929/0.165880. Grouped contractions can change
floating-point summation and the optimiser trajectory, while preserving the
loss and gradient algebra. These synthetic training measurements do not
measure event-generation efficiency; use benchmark_virtual_quality.py for
native loop calls, event distributions and envelope cost.

Implementation
--------------
virtual_pool.py: accepted-record pooling, serialization and diagnostics.
virtual_pool_polynomial.py: regression, matched MINT replay and CV selection.
virtual_pool_neural.py: conditional fitting, typed inputs and companion codec.
virtual_pooled.f90: generic coordinates, capture and frozen native inference.
mint_module.f90: residual envelopes, proposal sampling and generation diagnostics.
driver_mintMC.f and fks_singular.f: weighted Born screening, delayed exact
virtual evaluation and native contribution restoration.
